class.description:
Milestone 166 (decision 0307). Placed on a [Skeleton3D] after the modifiers that animate it (a [FellowMotionMatcher3D], say), it builds the skeleton's bodies at run time and drives them every physics step towards where the animation has their bones. The body then moves as the animation does, and also as the physics world pushes it. Set the skeleton's [member Skeleton3D.modifier_callback_mode_process] to [constant Skeleton3D.MODIFIER_CALLBACK_MODE_PROCESS_PHYSICS].
[b]The bodies[/b] are built on the stock [PhysicalBoneSimulator3D], which it adds to the skeleton after itself (named [code]FellowPhysicalBones[/code]), as the editor's Create Physical Skeleton makes them:
- a capsule along each bone at least [member min_bone_length] long, to its farthest child; the chain from the root to the head's end below 80 % of its height as thick as a torso (11 cm);
- a sphere for the root;
- a small body for every bone between two bodies, so that no joint spans a bone that turns;
- a sphere for a leaf bone named like a head when the bone above it has no body.
Their masses go by volume and together make [member total_mass]. Each body is joined to the nearest body up the bones: by a pin while the bodies are driven (their angles are the animation's), by a cone limited to [member swing_limit] and [member twist_limit] about the pose it went limp in once [member strength] is under 0.5. The bodies are on [member collision_layer] and meet [member collision_mask]; they do not meet one another.
[b]The drive[/b] runs each physics step. Each body's velocity goes towards the animation's own motion plus a share of the error ([member position_gain], [member rotation_gain] of it a step), less the gravity the step will add. The share of the step it takes is the strength cubed, so a weakened body is let go of. With nothing pushing, the bodies stay within millimetres of the animation.
[b]Hits[/b] ([method apply_hit]): the impulse goes to the body nearest the point. That body is weakened by [member hit_weakening], its neighbours by half of it a link (two links), and the weakness fades with [member recovery_halflife], so the body gives way and comes back.
[b]A ragdoll[/b]: [member strength] 0. Use Jolt Physics; Godot Physics' joints jitter there.

method.description:
Since [method reset_stats]:
- [code]tracking_error_mean_m[/code], [code]tracking_angle_mean_rad[/code], [code]tracking_error_max_m[/code]: the bodies' distance and angle from the animation's bones, while driven at full strength;
- [code]speed_max_m_s[/code]: the fastest body;
- [code]drive_usec_per_step[/code]: the drive's time a physics step;
- [code]rescues[/code]: bodies put back on their bones, held more than 0.75 m from them (behind a wall, under the ground);
- [code]bodies[/code], [code]weakness_max[/code].
